<header><p class="st-kicker">imperial city · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Free Imperial City of Wangen</h1><p class="st-meta">1286 CE – 1802 CE (approx.) · capital Wangen im Allgäu</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Small Catholic imperial city in the western Allgäu, a market for linen and scythes that kept its freedom until 1802.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wangen-im-allgau">Wangen</a> grew around a settlement linked to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/imperial-abbey-of-saint-gall">Imperial Abbey of Saint Gall</a>, which held rights there from the 9th century. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenstaufen">Hohenstaufen</a> developed the town, and after their fall it was recognised as an imperial city by Rudolf I in 1286.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>A council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burghers">burghers</a> ruled the town and its small territory. Linen weaving, scythe making and the market for the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allgau">Allgäu</a> were its main trades. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wangen-im-allgau">Wangen</a> remained Catholic during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> and was a member of the Swabian imperial circle. It suffered heavily in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-tyrants">the Thirty</a> Years War.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wangen-im-allgau">Wangen</a> lost its freedom to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> in 1802 and passed to Württemberg in 1810. It is now a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden-wurttemberg">Baden-Württemberg</a> with a well-preserved old centre, gate towers and painted houses.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/free-imperial-city-of-wangen.jpg" alt="Free Imperial City of Wangen" width="320" height="211"><figcaption>Image: Michael Lorer · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wangen_i_a_air16.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1286 CE – 1802 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>imperial city</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Wangen im Allgäu</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Swabian German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Germany</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Wangen im Allgäu</dd></dl></aside></div>
